A||N||5||gkozlov||87 Duke wont start||||1||
Z||000000||gkozlov||09-07-2000||09:33 PM||||I've replaced the spark plugs and wires. checked to make sure they are firing. All of them fired a nice pretty  blue spark. The car backfires a little.<P>I searched the forum and these were some of the things that were said to do. <P>What could it be. Is the 87 duke have timing gears or belt? How would I know if this is the problem? <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||gkozlov||09-07-2000||11:04 PM||||I can beg if need be. I know one of you guy and/or gals can set me in the right direction.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-07-2000||11:32 PM||||Is it getting gas?<P>yes they have the same timing gears as the older 4 cyls. Ignition timing is pulled off the crank not the cam. So if you think cam gears are hosed you'll need the vavle cover off to check it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phantom Rage||09-08-2000||06:22 AM||||If your starter is causing your motor to crank but it just won’t turn over then: <P>Yeah, like Ogre said maybe it's not getting gas.  To check if it is gas related first remove the air filters cover.  Now spray some starting fluid down the throttle body or pour in a little bit of gas.  Note:  The gas method is not really good for you motor since it is not atomized and will not distribute evenly.  Now have someone try to crank over the motor.  If it starts but dies in a few seconds, it might just very well be your fuel pump, or possibly a clogged fuel filter.<P>Good Luck <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000004||gkozlov||09-09-2000||10:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phantom Rage:<BR><B>If your starter is causing your motor to crank but it just won’t turn over then: <P>Yeah, like Ogre said maybe it's not getting gas.  To check if it is gas related first remove the air filters cover.  Now spray some starting fluid down the throttle body or pour in a little bit of gas.  Note:  The gas method is not really good for you motor since it is not atomized and will not distribute evenly.  Now have someone try to crank over the motor.  If it starts but dies in a few seconds, it might just very well be your fuel pump, or possibly a clogged fuel filter.<P>Good Luck </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can see the injector spraying a mist of gas when we try to start it. Does this  not eliminate the fuel system? NOTE: I am not a mechanic nor very mechanically enclined. I also want to note the car backfires a little when we attempt to start it. What does this mean? <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sign1_3.jpg">  <BR><A HREF="http://g.kozlov.tripod.com/gkozlov.htm" TARGET=_blank>GKozlovs Fiero Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-10-2000||12:33 AM||||look in my cave and carefully read the DIS notes. (link at bottom of this page)<P>sounds like either the PIM or the crank sensor may be shot. Last time mine did this it was the PIM. If you have to replace the PIM get the crank sensor to. You don't want to have to fool with it twice.<P>Be ready for some sticker shock. The PIM runs about $120. Coils are 30-50 each if you need them. The crank sensor was around $20.<P>You might want to consider getting the whole brick from a salvage yard. That would give you spare everything. This motor was used in a bunch of GM cars. The parts are all the same.||||reg||1
